In terms of gender, there were 320,285 males and 361,785 females, accounting for 47.0% and 53.0%
respectively of the total population. Females continued to outnumber males, and the gender ratio dropped
from 92.3 in 2011 to 88.5.
As shown in Chart 7 which illustrated the gender ratio by age group, the gender ratio of the youth
population (aged 0-14) was 108.8 as there were more male babies than female babies. On the other hand,
females predominated in all other age groups, with the gender ratios of the adult population (aged 15-64)
and the elderly population (aged 65 and above) standing at 84.2 and 93.3 respectively; this was attributed to
the dominant share of women among the incoming migrants and a longer lifespan of females.
With respect to the local population, the gender ratio fell from 92.7 in 2011 to 88.7. For the age groups
between 25 and 64 years old, the respective gender ratios were lower than the overall level of the local
population.
Chart 7: Gender Ratio by Age Group
1.3  Place of Birth
Incoming migrants are an essential component of the population of Macao. Results of the 2021
Population Census showed that the population born outside Macao increased by 22.8% from ten years ago
to 400,689, taking up 58.7% of the total population. Meanwhile, a total of 281,381 persons were born in
Macao, accounting for 41.3% of the total.
Analysed by place of birth of incoming migrants, those who were born in mainland China (298,887)
occupied 43.8% of the total population. Given the geographical proximity between Macao and Guangdong,
31.2% of the total population were born in Guangdong; those born in the nine Pearl River Delta cities of the
Greater Bay Area (189,458) constituted 27.8% of the total population.
On the other hand, 3.3% (22,199) and 0.3% (2,213) of the total pop

